<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Component property list for the per-version Fleet package.

    pkgbuild(8) discovers every bundle in the payload and, without this file,
    decides for itself what to do with each one. Both defaults it picks are
    wrong for a package whose whole purpose is to land at one exact path.

    BundleIsRelocatable defaults to true, which tells the installer to look for
    an existing copy of the bundle anywhere on the volume, by identifier, via
    Launch Services, and to install over that instead of over the install
    location the package names. A host that has ever had a Datadog Agent
    installed therefore gets the new payload written on top of the old bundle,
    outside the pool, and the version directory this package exists to create is
    left empty. The completeness check would then correctly refuse to name that
    version, so the experiment fails; but the previous version's bundle has
    already been overwritten, which is worse than a failed experiment.
    installer(8)'s target names a volume, not a directory, so the install
    location baked into the package is the only statement of where the payload
    goes, and relocation is precisely the mechanism that overrides it.

    BundleIsVersionChecked defaults to true, which tells the installer to skip
    the bundle if what is already on disk has a newer CFBundleVersion. That is
    right for an application a user might have upgraded by hand, and wrong here
    twice over: a Fleet downgrade is a normal, deliberate operation, since it is
    what a revert to an older stable version is; and the decision of which
    version a host should run belongs to the backend, not to a version-string
    comparison made by the installer. A silently skipped payload also leaves
    pkgbuild reporting success over an empty version directory.

    Both keys are therefore stated explicitly and false. Every bundle in the
    payload needs an entry: pkgbuild only honours the settings for bundles this
    file names, and falls back to the defaults for the rest.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<array>
    <dict>
        <key>BundleHasStrictIdentifier</key>
        <false/>
        <key>BundleIsRelocatable</key>
        <false/>
        <key>BundleIsVersionChecked</key>
        <false/>
        <key>BundleOverwriteAction</key>
        <string>upgrade</string>
        <!-- Relative to the payload root, which is the version directory. -->
        <key>RootRelativeBundlePath</key>
        <string>Datadog Agent.app</string>
    </dict>
</array>
</plist>
